      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "F5",
      "statement": "Shared devices on routes. Alert 1: device D-568 sent all six onward transfers on its routes (T0007, T0014 from A-9278; T0008, T0015 from A-5092; T0009, T0016 from A-2897). So A-9278, A-5092 and A-2897 are the route accounts that sent from a device another route account also used. Alert 2: no device was used by two or more accounts on its routes. The only other device in the dataset used by more than one account, D-619 (A-2952, A-9652), is on neither alert's routes.",
      "claim_ids": [
        "s1",
        "a1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "F6",
      "statement": "What distinguishes the alerts. Both meet the same threshold (6 senders, 6 transfers, one-hour window), but the recorded relationships differ. (a) Alert 1 passes on all its contributing credits; Alert 2 passes on only half. (b) Alert 1 runs 3 links deep through A-5092 and A-2897 to A-4989; Alert 2 is 1 link deep to A-1415. (c) On Alert 1's route each hop follows within 140000–195000 ms, and each forwarded amount is slightly smaller (138000→135000→132000; 210000→206000→202000). (d) All of Alert 1's onward transfers come from one device, D-568, used by three different route accounts; Alert 2's route has no shared device. (e) Alert 1's credits are varied and large (total 357000); Alert 2's are six equal 2500 credits (total 15000). None of the downstream accounts (A-5092, A-2897, A-4989, A-1415) has a fan-in alert of its own. Interpretation: Alert 1 sits on a fast, multi-hop, single-device chain, so it warrants priority review. Alert 2 is, on the recorded evidence, a simple collection with one partial forward, and the fan-in alert alone sets it apart.",
